schema: encyclopedia-entry
object:
  title: 'Federation and Portability: Building a Commons That Outlives Its Tools'
  type: encyclopedia-entry
  page_type: framework
  summary: >-
    Argues that a knowledge commons needs both portability (moving your data
    between tools/people/orgs without rot) and federation (sharing across
    groups without merging into one database). Recommends a "portability
    floor" of plain Markdown in Git (readable, diffable, greppable,
    vendor-independent) with structure kept in frontmatter, then layers
    federation on top via named public/internal/private boundaries and
    stable references — from ad hoc shared folders up to protocols like
    KOI-net (RIDs) or ActivityPub (Mastodon).
  audience: small-team leads who work in docs/GitHub but aren't ontologists
  known_tensions:
    - >-
      protocol-maturity-gap — ActivityPub/Mastodon federates social identity
      well but, as of 2025, doesn't yet portable-move actual post content
  related_concepts:
    - portability
    - federation
    - reference-identifier-rid
  related_resources:
    - obsidian
    - koi-net
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/federation-portability.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
